## Why retry loops are difficult to diagnose

A retry is useful only when the next attempt has a meaningful chance of producing a different result. In production agent workflows, repeated attempts can otherwise hide the original failure while increasing latency and cost.

Start with [LLM tracing](/blog/llm-tracing-explained) to preserve the attempt sequence, then use an [AI agent reliability strategy](/blog/ai-agent-reliability-failures-retries-guardrails) to define bounded recovery behavior.

## Reconstruct the decision path

Start with the complete trace. Identify the first failed tool call, the condition that selected another attempt, and any state that changed between attempts.

1. Compare tool inputs across attempts.
2. Check whether the error is transient or deterministic.
3. Confirm the retry count and backoff policy.
4. Inspect the final user-visible outcome.

> A bounded retry policy should make the stop condition as observable as the retry condition.

## Verify the fix

Add the failure case to an evaluation dataset, run the old and new policies against the same input, and compare outcome quality, latency, cost, and failure classification before promotion.
